WEBVTT

00:00.024 --> 00:02.190
What I want to do in this
video is talk a little bit

00:02.190 --> 00:04.560
about polynomial end behavior.

00:04.560 --> 00:06.310
And this is really
just talking about what

00:06.310 --> 00:11.230
happens to a polynomial if
as x becomes really large

00:11.230 --> 00:13.250
or really, really,
really negative.

00:13.250 --> 00:17.700
For example, we're familiar
with quadratic polynomials where

00:17.700 --> 00:23.440
y is equal to ax
squared plus bx plus c.

00:23.440 --> 00:26.160
We know that if a
is greater than 0,

00:26.160 --> 00:28.811
this is going to be an upward
opening parabola of some kind.

00:28.811 --> 00:30.560
So it's going to look
something like that,

00:30.560 --> 00:34.240
the graph of this equation,
or of this function,

00:34.240 --> 00:35.200
you could say.

00:35.200 --> 00:38.389
And if a is less
than 0, it's going

00:38.389 --> 00:39.805
to be a downward
opening parabola.

00:43.270 --> 00:45.570
We've spent less time with
third degree polynomials,

00:45.570 --> 00:47.310
but we've also seen
those a little bit.

00:47.310 --> 00:51.020
So for example, if you have
the third degree polynomial,

00:51.020 --> 00:59.330
y is equal to ax to the third
plus bx squared plus cx plus d,

00:59.330 --> 01:05.500
if a is greater than 0-- I don't
want to use that brown color.

01:05.500 --> 01:11.710
If a is greater than 0,
when x is really, really,

01:11.710 --> 01:14.090
really negative, this whole
thing is going to be really,

01:14.090 --> 01:15.360
really, really negative.

01:15.360 --> 01:19.730
And then it's going to increase
as x becomes less negative.

01:19.730 --> 01:21.540
It's going do
something-- it might

01:21.540 --> 01:23.630
do a little bit of
funky stuff in between.

01:23.630 --> 01:26.400
But then as x becomes more
and more and more positive,

01:26.400 --> 01:29.930
it will become more and more
and more positive as well.

01:29.930 --> 01:35.900
So it might look something like
this when a is greater than 0.

01:35.900 --> 01:37.800
But what about when
a is less than 0?

01:37.800 --> 01:40.040
Well then, just like
here, we would flip it.

01:40.040 --> 01:46.180
We would flip it so that
if a is less than 0,

01:46.180 --> 01:48.290
when x is really
negative, you're

01:48.290 --> 01:50.282
going to multiply that
times a negative a

01:50.282 --> 01:51.990
and you're going to
get a positive value.

01:51.990 --> 01:54.100
So it's going to look
something like this.

01:54.100 --> 01:55.980
And then it's going
to go like this.

01:55.980 --> 01:58.740
It might do a little bit of this
type of business in between.

01:58.740 --> 02:01.950
But then its end behavior,
it starts decreasing again.

02:01.950 --> 02:03.540
It starts decreasing.

02:03.540 --> 02:05.660
So when we talk
about end behavior,

02:05.660 --> 02:08.330
we're talking about the idea
of what is this function?

02:08.330 --> 02:11.190
What does this polynomial do
as x becomes really, really,

02:11.190 --> 02:13.300
really, really positive
and as x becomes really,

02:13.300 --> 02:14.680
really, really, really negative?

02:14.680 --> 02:18.065
And kind of fully recognizing
that some weird things might

02:18.065 --> 02:19.190
be happening in the middle.

02:19.190 --> 02:20.689
But we just want
to think about what

02:20.689 --> 02:23.440
happens at extreme values of x.

02:23.440 --> 02:25.320
Now obviously for
the second degree

02:25.320 --> 02:28.630
polynomial nothing really
weird happens in the middle.

02:28.630 --> 02:30.380
But for a third
degree polynomial,

02:30.380 --> 02:33.600
we sort of see that
some interesting things

02:33.600 --> 02:35.320
can start happening
in the middle.

02:35.320 --> 02:38.650
But the end behavior for
third degree polynomial

02:38.650 --> 02:41.300
is that if a is
greater than 0-- we're

02:41.300 --> 02:43.540
starting really small,
really low values--

02:43.540 --> 02:46.870
and as a becomes positive,
we get to really high values.

02:46.870 --> 02:49.130
If a is less than 0
we have the opposite.

02:49.130 --> 02:51.620
And these are kind of the two
prototypes for polynomials.

02:51.620 --> 02:54.160
Because from there we can
start thinking about any degree

02:54.160 --> 02:55.380
polynomial.

02:55.380 --> 02:59.990
So let's just think about the
situation of a fourth degree

02:59.990 --> 03:01.280
polynomial.

03:01.280 --> 03:06.600
So let's say y is equal
to ax to the fourth power

03:06.600 --> 03:13.360
plus bx to the third plus cx
squared plus dx plus-- I don't

03:13.360 --> 03:17.080
want to write e because e has
other meanings in mathematics.

03:17.080 --> 03:21.704
I'll say plus-- I'm really
running out of letters here.

03:21.704 --> 03:23.870
I'll just use f, although
this isn't the function f.

03:23.870 --> 03:27.230
This is just a constant
f right over here.

03:27.230 --> 03:29.860
So let's just think about
what this might look like.

03:29.860 --> 03:31.409
Let's think about
its end behavior,

03:31.409 --> 03:33.700
and we could think about it
relative to a second degree

03:33.700 --> 03:35.040
polynomial.

03:35.040 --> 03:38.690
So its end behavior, if x is
really, really, really, really

03:38.690 --> 03:41.400
negative, x to the fourth is
still going to be positive.

03:41.400 --> 03:46.345
And if a is greater than 0
when x is really, really,

03:46.345 --> 03:48.470
really negative, we're
going to have really, really

03:48.470 --> 03:52.920
positive values, just
like a second degree.

03:52.920 --> 03:55.274
And when x is really
positive, same thing.

03:55.274 --> 03:56.940
x to the fourth is
going to be positive,

03:56.940 --> 03:58.870
times a is still
going to be positive.

03:58.870 --> 04:02.050
So its end behavior
is going to look

04:02.050 --> 04:05.280
very similar to a second
degree polynomial.

04:05.280 --> 04:07.280
Now, it might do--
in fact it probably

04:07.280 --> 04:09.060
will do some funky
stuff in between.

04:09.060 --> 04:15.570
It might do something that looks
kind of like that in between.

04:15.570 --> 04:18.220
But we care about
the end behavior.

04:18.220 --> 04:20.430
I guess you could call
the stuff that I've dotted

04:20.430 --> 04:23.050
lined in the middle, this is
called the non-end behavior,

04:23.050 --> 04:24.390
the middle behavior.

04:24.390 --> 04:26.680
This will obviously be
different than a second degree

04:26.680 --> 04:27.540
polynomial.

04:27.540 --> 04:30.560
But what happens at the
ends will be the same.

04:30.560 --> 04:32.530
And the reason why, when
you square something,

04:32.530 --> 04:34.321
or you raise something
to the fourth power,

04:34.321 --> 04:37.470
you raise anything to any even
power for a very large-- as

04:37.470 --> 04:40.540
long as a is greater than 0,
for very large positive values,

04:40.540 --> 04:42.040
you're going to get
positive values.

04:42.040 --> 04:44.182
And for very large
negative values,

04:44.182 --> 04:46.140
you're going to get very
large positive values.

04:46.140 --> 04:48.665
You take a negative number,
raise it to the fourth power,

04:48.665 --> 04:52.020
or the second power, you're
going to get a positive value.

04:52.020 --> 04:54.300
Likewise, if a is
less than 0, you're

04:54.300 --> 04:57.070
going to have very similar
end behavior to this case.

04:57.070 --> 05:01.520
For a polynomial where
the highest degree

05:01.520 --> 05:06.980
term is even-- so this is
a is less than 0-- your end

05:06.980 --> 05:09.259
behavior when a is really,
really, really, really

05:09.259 --> 05:11.050
negative, this thing
is going to be really,

05:11.050 --> 05:12.150
really, really positive.

05:12.150 --> 05:13.850
We're going to be multiplying
it times a negative,

05:13.850 --> 05:14.750
so it's going to
be really, really,

05:14.750 --> 05:16.040
really, really negative.

05:16.040 --> 05:17.350
So it'll look like this.

05:17.350 --> 05:20.992
And likewise, when x is really,
really, really positive,

05:20.992 --> 05:21.950
you get the same thing.

05:21.950 --> 05:24.366
Because you're going to be
multiplying a positive times a,

05:24.366 --> 05:26.010
which is negative,
and in between it

05:26.010 --> 05:30.140
might be doing
something like that.

05:30.140 --> 05:32.090
But its end
behavior, you see, is

05:32.090 --> 05:35.530
very similar to a second
degree polynomial.

05:35.530 --> 05:40.320
So if you ignore this, its
end behavior is very similar.

05:40.320 --> 05:43.330
Now the same is true
for a fifth degree

05:43.330 --> 05:45.530
if you were to compare
it to a third degree.

05:45.530 --> 05:51.040
And the overall idea here is
what happens to this value when

05:51.040 --> 05:54.000
we get really large x's
or really small x's?

05:54.000 --> 05:55.590
Are we taking it
to an even power?

05:55.590 --> 05:58.240
In which case for either really
negative values or really

05:58.240 --> 06:00.760
positive values we're going
to get positive values.

06:00.760 --> 06:02.740
And then it depends what
our coefficient a is.

06:02.740 --> 06:07.420
Or are we taking
it to an odd power?

06:07.420 --> 06:09.380
So the general
idea-- and actually

06:09.380 --> 06:12.460
let me just do a fifth degree,
just to make the clear.

06:12.460 --> 06:14.100
So if I had something
of the form y

06:14.100 --> 06:19.209
is equal to ax to the fifth
plus bx to the fourth plus--

06:19.209 --> 06:21.750
and it just went all the way--
I won't even have to write it.

06:21.750 --> 06:27.139
This thing, if a
is greater than 0,

06:27.139 --> 06:28.430
would look something like this.

06:28.430 --> 06:34.420
Its end behavior is very similar
to a third degree polynomial

06:34.420 --> 06:35.990
where a is greater than 0.

06:35.990 --> 06:37.310
At the end it would do this.

06:37.310 --> 06:45.470
Now, it might do kind of
some craziness like this.

06:45.470 --> 06:46.810
I have to get this right.

06:46.810 --> 06:51.580
So 1, 2, 3-- it might do some
craziness like this in between.

06:51.580 --> 06:56.080
But then for really large x's
it will look the same as ax

06:56.080 --> 06:59.050
to the third when a
is greater than 0.

06:59.050 --> 07:02.830
So once again, very,
very similar end behavior

07:02.830 --> 07:05.060
when a is greater than
0, and very similar

07:05.060 --> 07:07.140
end behavior when
a is less than 0.

07:07.140 --> 07:08.140
It would look like this.

07:08.140 --> 07:10.960
At the ends at a
negative value it

07:10.960 --> 07:12.699
will be positive
because this part

07:12.699 --> 07:13.990
is going to be really negative.

07:13.990 --> 07:16.073
But then it's going to be
multiplied by a negative

07:16.073 --> 07:16.830
to get a positive.

07:16.830 --> 07:20.170
And for really positive values
of x, it will be negative.

07:20.170 --> 07:23.270
Because once again, this a
term is going to be negative.

07:23.270 --> 07:25.500
And then what it does
in between-- at least

07:25.500 --> 07:27.030
for the sake of
this video-- we're

07:27.030 --> 07:28.590
not really thinking about.

07:28.590 --> 07:30.687
So the big takeaway
here-- and this

07:30.687 --> 07:32.270
is kind of a little
bit of a drum roll

07:32.270 --> 07:34.560
here, we're talking
about end behavior--

07:34.560 --> 07:40.050
if you're looking at an
even degree polynomial

07:40.050 --> 07:43.120
it's going to have end
behavior like a second degree

07:43.120 --> 07:43.680
polynomial.

07:43.680 --> 07:45.150
If you ignore what happens
in the middle, what

07:45.150 --> 07:47.760
happens at really negative
values of x and really positive

07:47.760 --> 07:50.240
values of x is going to be
very similar to a second degree

07:50.240 --> 07:51.360
polynomial.

07:51.360 --> 07:54.460
And if your degree
is odd, you're

07:54.460 --> 07:57.950
going to have very similar
end behavior to a third degree

07:57.950 --> 07:58.737
polynomial.

07:58.737 --> 08:00.820
You might do all sorts of
craziness in the middle,

08:00.820 --> 08:03.695
but given for a given a,
whether it's greater than 0

08:03.695 --> 08:06.920
or less than 0, you will
have end behavior like this,

08:06.920 --> 08:09.800
or end behavior like that.
